1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:01,910
Léa: Hello everyone,

2
00:00:01,910 --> 00:00:04,240
and welcome to The Cozy French!

3
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:07,080
I'm Léa, and I'm happy to be back with you

4
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:10,000
for a new conversation in French.

5
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:11,980
Wherever you are, take a moment

6
00:00:11,980 --> 00:00:14,400
to get comfortable.

7
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:15,900
Mathieu: And I'm Mathieu!

8
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:17,500
Hello, dear friends!

9
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:19,410
Whether you're making your coffee,

10
00:00:19,410 --> 00:00:21,040
out for a walk

11
00:00:21,040 --> 00:00:22,440
or on public transport,

12
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:25,160
thank you for spending this time with us.

13
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:26,450
Today, Léa,

14
00:00:26,450 --> 00:00:29,270
our topic is rather ambitious, isn't it?

15
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:32,380
Léa: Yes! We're going to talk about this phrase:

16
00:00:32,380 --> 00:00:34,900
"It's time to change your life."

17
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:36,200
But don't worry,

18
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:37,600
we're not going to advise you

19
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:39,400
to quit your job tomorrow

20
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:41,800
or move to the other side of the world.

21
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:44,310
Mathieu: Good, because tomorrow

22
00:00:44,310 --> 00:00:46,500
I have to water my plants.

23
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:49,290
And some of them would never forgive me

24
00:00:49,290 --> 00:00:51,100
for a hasty departure.

25
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:54,160
Léa: You see, change doesn't necessarily need

26
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:55,970
to be spectacular.

27
00:00:56,020 --> 00:00:59,300
Sometimes it starts with a small question:

28
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:00,660
"Does my current life

29
00:01:00,660 --> 00:01:01,850
still suit me?"

30
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:03,410
Mathieu: That's interesting,

31
00:01:03,410 --> 00:01:05,700
because you can have a decent job,

32
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:08,960
reassuring habits, and still feel

33
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:10,900
that something is missing.

34
00:01:10,900 --> 00:01:14,350
Léa: Exactly. Today we'll think about

35
00:01:14,350 --> 00:01:16,600
what makes us want to change,

36
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:18,330
the fears that hold us back

37
00:01:18,330 --> 00:01:19,400
and the first steps

38
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:21,870
we can take without turning everything upside down.

39
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:24,200
Mathieu: And as always, we'll discover

40
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:26,410
some useful French expressions,

41
00:01:26,410 --> 00:01:28,600
with simple examples.

42
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:30,510
If you're learning French,

43
00:01:30,510 --> 00:01:33,600
don't try to understand every word.

44
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:36,500
Just follow our conversation calmly.

45
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:39,400
Léa: By the way, tell us in the comments:

46
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:41,890
what little thing would you like to change

47
00:01:41,890 --> 00:01:43,350
in your daily life?

48
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:45,800
Your answers can be very simple.

49
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:48,100
And if you enjoy our chats,

50
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:50,770
remember to subscribe to The Cozy French.

51
00:01:50,820 --> 00:01:52,900
Mathieu: OK, Léa, I have a question.

52
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:54,260
How do you know

53
00:01:54,260 --> 00:01:56,900
that you really need a change,

54
00:01:56,900 --> 00:01:58,900
and that you're not just going through

55
00:01:58,900 --> 00:02:00,350
a bad week?

56
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:02,920
Léa: I think you have to watch for the feelings

57
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:04,100
that keep coming back.

58
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:05,530
A difficult day

59
00:02:05,530 --> 00:02:07,600
is just a difficult day.

60
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:09,900
But if, for months,

61
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:12,200
you keep feeling the same emptiness,

62
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:15,100
that feeling may deserve your attention.

63
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:18,340
Mathieu: I know that feeling. A few years ago,

64
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:20,800
my weeks were so alike

65
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:22,220
that I couldn't even

66
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:24,700
tell a Tuesday from a Thursday.

67
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:25,950
Léa: Were you working a lot?

68
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:27,520
Mathieu: Yes, but above all,

69
00:02:27,520 --> 00:02:30,300
I always followed the same schedule.

70
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:35,700
Work, groceries, dinner, TV, sleep.

71
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:38,340
I didn't hate that life,

72
00:02:38,340 --> 00:02:41,100
but I felt like I was on autopilot.

73
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:43,010
Léa: "Être en pilote automatique"

74
00:02:43,010 --> 00:02:45,370
means doing things mechanically,

75
00:02:45,420 --> 00:02:48,400
without really choosing or thinking.

76
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:51,600
You keep going because you're used to it.

77
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:53,400
Mathieu: That's exactly it.

78
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:55,280
And when someone asked me

79
00:02:55,280 --> 00:02:57,960
what I wanted to change, I'd answer:

80
00:02:57,970 --> 00:03:00,250
"I don't want this routine anymore."

81
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:02,450
But I couldn't explain

82
00:03:02,450 --> 00:03:04,600
what I wanted instead.

83
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:06,600
Léa: That's a first clue.

84
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:08,510
It's useful to recognise

85
00:03:08,510 --> 00:03:10,200
what no longer suits us.

86
00:03:10,200 --> 00:03:12,600
But you also have to ask yourself:

87
00:03:12,630 --> 00:03:14,090
"What would I like to get back

88
00:03:14,090 --> 00:03:15,270
into my week?"

89
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:16,600
Mathieu: More free time,

90
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:19,650
more encounters, more creativity?

91
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:23,400
Léa: Yes. Or simply more peace and quiet.

92
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:25,310
Change sometimes begins

93
00:03:25,310 --> 00:03:27,500
when we identify what we're missing,

94
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:29,410
not when we find a perfect plan

95
00:03:29,410 --> 00:03:31,100
for the next ten years.

96
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:34,400
Mathieu: Do you know what I missed? Photography.

97
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:35,760
When I was a student,

98
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:37,700
I loved taking my camera

99
00:03:37,700 --> 00:03:39,610
and heading off to explore the streets

100
00:03:39,610 --> 00:03:41,700
with no particular destination.

101
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:43,620
Léa: I didn't know you were passionate

102
00:03:43,620 --> 00:03:44,770
about photography!

103
00:03:44,820 --> 00:03:46,500
Mathieu: I really was.

104
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:48,010
Then work took up

105
00:03:48,010 --> 00:03:50,100
more and more room.

106
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:53,100
I told myself I'd get back to it later,

107
00:03:53,100 --> 00:03:54,900
when I had more time.

108
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:57,350
Léa: And did that "later" last long?

109
00:03:57,400 --> 00:04:01,000
Mathieu: Almost four years. One Sunday,

110
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,800
I found an old box of photos.

111
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:05,800
You could see colourful façades,

112
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:09,200
a market, a bike left against a wall.

113
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:10,870
Nothing extraordinary,

114
00:04:10,920 --> 00:04:12,980
but I remembered perfectly

115
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:15,700
how much I'd enjoyed taking them.

116
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:17,050
Léa: And then?

117
00:04:17,100 --> 00:04:18,500
Mathieu: The following weekend,

118
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:20,800
I went out again with my camera.

119
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:21,840
I didn't try

120
00:04:21,840 --> 00:04:23,900
to take magnificent photos.

121
00:04:23,900 --> 00:04:26,010
I just walked for an hour,

122
00:04:26,010 --> 00:04:29,100
looking at things differently.

123
00:04:29,100 --> 00:04:31,450
Léa: And how did you feel?

124
00:04:31,500 --> 00:04:33,700
Mathieu: As if I'd found a part of myself

125
00:04:33,700 --> 00:04:35,500
that I had set aside.

126
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:37,970
It was a tiny decision,

127
00:04:37,970 --> 00:04:40,520
but it changed my Saturdays.

128
00:04:40,570 --> 00:04:42,200
Léa: I love that story.

129
00:04:42,200 --> 00:04:43,930
Sometimes, changing your life

130
00:04:43,930 --> 00:04:47,000
doesn't mean becoming someone else.

131
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,300
It means making room again

132
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:53,600
for something you already loved.

133
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:56,250
Mathieu: Yes, but sometimes what we used to love

134
00:04:56,250 --> 00:04:58,900
we don't enjoy anymore.

135
00:04:58,900 --> 00:05:01,650
Are we allowed to change our minds?

136
00:05:01,700 --> 00:05:04,700
Léa: Of course! Our desires evolve.

137
00:05:04,700 --> 00:05:07,100
The job we wanted at twenty,

138
00:05:07,100 --> 00:05:09,970
the habits that once suited us

139
00:05:10,020 --> 00:05:12,300
or the projects we cared about

140
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:14,610
may no longer match

141
00:05:14,610 --> 00:05:16,600
our current needs.

142
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:19,700
Mathieu: And yet it can be hard to accept.

143
00:05:19,700 --> 00:05:21,060
We tell ourselves:

144
00:05:21,060 --> 00:05:23,200
"I spent years building this life.

145
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:25,600
I can't change now."

146
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:28,700
Léa: But past efforts aren't erased

147
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:31,100
when we take a new direction.

148
00:05:31,100 --> 00:05:33,000
They helped us learn,

149
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,200
develop skills

150
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:37,470
and get to know ourselves better.

151
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:39,400
Mathieu: So an old decision

152
00:05:39,400 --> 00:05:41,700
wasn't necessarily a bad one.

153
00:05:41,700 --> 00:05:44,200
It may have suited the person

154
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:46,400
we were at the time.

155
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:48,950
Léa: Exactly. That's what we sometimes call

156
00:05:48,950 --> 00:05:51,850
"revoir ses priorités" — rethinking priorities.

157
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:54,100
We look at what matters today

158
00:05:54,100 --> 00:05:55,130
and we accept

159
00:05:55,130 --> 00:05:58,400
that the answer may not be yesterday's.

160
00:05:58,400 --> 00:06:01,610
Absolutely. When you know a situation,

161
00:06:01,610 --> 00:06:03,370
even an imperfect one,

162
00:06:03,420 --> 00:06:05,700
it can seem more reassuring

163
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:08,100
than an unknown possibility.

164
00:06:08,100 --> 00:06:11,000
Mathieu: We wonder: "What if I'm wrong?

165
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:12,450
What if I regret it?

166
00:06:12,500 --> 00:06:15,050
What if I'm not skilled enough?"

167
00:06:15,100 --> 00:06:18,700
And in the end, we'd rather not try anything.

168
00:06:18,700 --> 00:06:21,400
Léa: This caution isn't always a bad thing.

169
00:06:21,400 --> 00:06:24,800
Some decisions have real consequences.

170
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:26,200
But you don't have to

171
00:06:26,200 --> 00:06:28,300
make a huge decision

172
00:06:28,300 --> 00:06:31,070
to explore a new desire.

173
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:34,350
Mathieu: You mean we can try on a small scale?

174
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:38,470
Léa: Exactly. I know a woman, Camille,

175
00:06:38,470 --> 00:06:40,770
who loved cooking and dreamed

176
00:06:40,770 --> 00:06:44,600
of sharing her recipes in small workshops.

177
00:06:44,600 --> 00:06:46,040
Mathieu: Now that's an activity

178
00:06:46,040 --> 00:06:48,800
I enthusiastically support.

179
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:51,400
Especially if I get to taste the results.

180
00:06:51,400 --> 00:06:53,000
Léa: I didn't doubt it for a second!

181
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:55,700
But Camille always had an excuse

182
00:06:55,700 --> 00:06:57,470
not to start.

183
00:06:57,520 --> 00:06:58,880
She thought she needed

184
00:06:58,880 --> 00:07:00,800
a professional kitchen,

185
00:07:00,800 --> 00:07:02,710
a perfect website

186
00:07:02,710 --> 00:07:05,200
and lots of experience.

187
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:08,300
Mathieu: So the project became so big

188
00:07:08,300 --> 00:07:11,000
that she couldn't take the first step.

189
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:14,300
Léa: Yes. One day, she invited four friends

190
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:15,600
to teach them

191
00:07:15,600 --> 00:07:17,800
how to make a family recipe.

192
00:07:17,800 --> 00:07:20,100
She was afraid of forgetting something.

193
00:07:20,100 --> 00:07:24,170
And sure enough, she forgot to buy lemons!

194
00:07:24,220 --> 00:07:27,750
Mathieu: Oh no! And did the class stop?

195
00:07:27,800 --> 00:07:31,700
Léa: Not at all. They improvised, they laughed,

196
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:34,800
and everyone had a great time.

197
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:37,700
At the end, two people asked her

198
00:07:37,700 --> 00:07:40,900
when she'd hold another workshop.

199
00:07:40,900 --> 00:07:43,900
Mathieu: So that little experience taught her

200
00:07:43,900 --> 00:07:46,190
more than months

201
00:07:46,190 --> 00:07:49,400
of imagining every possible problem.

202
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:51,820
Léa: Exactly. She didn't open

203
00:07:51,820 --> 00:07:53,870
a cooking school the next day.

204
00:07:53,920 --> 00:07:55,290
But she discovered

205
00:07:55,290 --> 00:07:57,300
that passing on her knowledge

206
00:07:57,300 --> 00:07:59,700
truly made her happy.

207
00:07:59,700 --> 00:08:01,610
Mathieu: That's a good difference

208
00:08:01,610 --> 00:08:05,400
between imagining an activity and doing it.

209
00:08:05,400 --> 00:08:06,600
In our heads,

210
00:08:06,600 --> 00:08:10,100
everything can seem wonderful or disastrous.

211
00:08:10,100 --> 00:08:12,010
Experience gives us

212
00:08:12,010 --> 00:08:14,300
more concrete information.

213
00:08:14,300 --> 00:08:16,300
Léa: Yes. And sometimes,

214
00:08:16,300 --> 00:08:18,600
experience also teaches us

215
00:08:18,600 --> 00:08:20,800
that we don't like an activity as much

216
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:21,870
as we thought.

217
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:24,600
Mathieu: Oh, that reminds me of my pottery classes!

218
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:26,350
Léa: You did pottery?

219
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:28,100
Mathieu: Three sessions.

220
00:08:28,100 --> 00:08:29,830
In my imagination,

221
00:08:29,830 --> 00:08:33,100
I was creating magnificent vases.

222
00:08:33,100 --> 00:08:34,590
In reality,

223
00:08:34,590 --> 00:08:38,600
I made a small, completely lopsided bowl.

224
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:40,200
Léa: That's OK!

225
00:08:40,200 --> 00:08:43,300
You'd tried something new.

226
00:08:43,300 --> 00:08:46,200
Mathieu: Yes, and above all, I discovered

227
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:49,100
that I preferred admiring ceramics

228
00:08:49,100 --> 00:08:51,170
rather than making them.

229
00:08:51,220 --> 00:08:52,900
I didn't continue the classes,

230
00:08:52,900 --> 00:08:55,480
but I didn't see the experience

231
00:08:55,480 --> 00:08:56,700
as a failure.

232
00:08:56,700 --> 00:08:59,100
Léa: That's a useful lesson.

233
00:08:59,100 --> 00:09:01,300
Trying a new direction

234
00:09:01,300 --> 00:09:04,900
doesn't mean you have to stay there forever.

235
00:09:04,900 --> 00:09:07,470
You can test, learn,

236
00:09:07,470 --> 00:09:09,400
then adjust your choice.

237
00:09:09,400 --> 00:09:10,850
Mathieu: And my bowl?

238
00:09:10,900 --> 00:09:14,200
Now it's used to hold paper clips.

239
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:17,500
In the end, it found its place in the world.

240
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:20,970
Léa: A perfectly successful career change!

241
00:09:21,020 --> 00:09:26,000
Mathieu: OK, but let's imagine a more serious situation.

242
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,200
Someone wants to change careers,

243
00:09:28,200 --> 00:09:30,110
but they have bills to pay,

244
00:09:30,110 --> 00:09:33,100
a family and responsibilities.

245
00:09:33,100 --> 00:09:35,900
They can't just quit their job.

246
00:09:35,900 --> 00:09:37,200
Léa: Of course.

247
00:09:37,200 --> 00:09:39,500
Changing your life isn't that simple

248
00:09:39,500 --> 00:09:40,900
for everyone.

249
00:09:40,900 --> 00:09:42,810
Financial, family or personal constraints

250
00:09:42,810 --> 00:09:45,800
all count.

251
00:09:45,800 --> 00:09:48,170
And staying in your current situation

252
00:09:48,220 --> 00:09:50,210
can be a reasonable decision

253
00:09:50,210 --> 00:09:52,500
while you prepare something else.

254
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:56,200
Mathieu: So you can start changing direction

255
00:09:56,200 --> 00:09:58,110
without making

256
00:09:58,110 --> 00:10:00,400
a big visible change right away.

257
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:04,300
Léa: Exactly. You can look into a profession,

258
00:10:04,300 --> 00:10:06,800
meet people who work in it,

259
00:10:06,800 --> 00:10:11,200
take a course or start saving.

260
00:10:11,200 --> 00:10:13,750
These steps may be discreet,

261
00:10:13,750 --> 00:10:15,530
but they're real.

262
00:10:15,580 --> 00:10:18,300
Mathieu: I like this idea of quiet progress.

263
00:10:18,300 --> 00:10:21,300
Sometimes nobody sees what's changing,

264
00:10:21,300 --> 00:10:24,500
but we know we're preparing something.

265
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:26,800
Léa: Yes. And you have to be careful

266
00:10:26,800 --> 00:10:28,950
not to compare your pace

267
00:10:28,950 --> 00:10:30,600
with other people's.

268
00:10:30,600 --> 00:10:32,790
Some people can make

269
00:10:32,790 --> 00:10:34,300
quick decisions.

270
00:10:34,300 --> 00:10:36,800
Others need several months

271
00:10:36,800 --> 00:10:38,700
or several years.

272
00:10:38,700 --> 00:10:41,700
Yes. We want to have all the answers

273
00:10:41,700 --> 00:10:44,170
before doing anything at all.

274
00:10:44,220 --> 00:10:46,200
But for many projects,

275
00:10:46,200 --> 00:10:48,130
confidence also comes

276
00:10:48,130 --> 00:10:51,000
from our first experiences.

277
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,700
But Mathieu, there's one question

278
00:10:53,700 --> 00:10:54,900
we mustn't forget.

279
00:10:54,900 --> 00:10:57,100
How do we know if we need

280
00:10:57,100 --> 00:10:59,000
to change something,

281
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:02,150
or simply to rest?

282
00:11:02,200 --> 00:11:03,400
Mathieu: Oh yes.

283
00:11:03,400 --> 00:11:05,100
Because when I sleep badly

284
00:11:05,100 --> 00:11:06,700
for several nights,

285
00:11:06,700 --> 00:11:07,910
I suddenly find

286
00:11:07,910 --> 00:11:10,830
that everything in my life is unbearable.

287
00:11:10,880 --> 00:11:14,270
Léa: That's exactly why it's better to avoid

288
00:11:14,270 --> 00:11:15,600
jumping to big conclusions

289
00:11:15,600 --> 00:11:18,400
from a single bad day.

290
00:11:18,400 --> 00:11:20,310
You can watch for the feelings

291
00:11:20,310 --> 00:11:22,200
that come back regularly,

292
00:11:22,200 --> 00:11:24,250
give yourself time and talk

293
00:11:24,250 --> 00:11:26,400
to someone you trust.

294
00:11:26,400 --> 00:11:28,330
Mathieu: Maybe I don't need

295
00:11:28,330 --> 00:11:29,700
a new job.

296
00:11:29,700 --> 00:11:31,060
Maybe what I need is

297
00:11:31,060 --> 00:11:34,000
better boundaries in my current job.

298
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:37,840
Léa: Exactly. Or an honest conversation,

299
00:11:37,840 --> 00:11:39,170
some free time,

300
00:11:39,220 --> 00:11:43,200
or simply getting back to a pleasant activity.

301
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:44,080
Change

302
00:11:44,080 --> 00:11:46,800
may be smaller than we thought.

303
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:48,330
Mathieu: "Poser des limites" — setting boundaries —

304
00:11:48,330 --> 00:11:51,140
means learning to say what you can accept

305
00:11:51,140 --> 00:11:52,550
or not, right?

306
00:11:52,600 --> 00:11:54,820
Léa: Yes. For example:

307
00:11:54,820 --> 00:11:57,190
"I can help you tomorrow, but tonight

308
00:11:57,190 --> 00:11:59,600
I need to rest."

309
00:11:59,600 --> 00:12:00,960
It's a way

310
00:12:00,960 --> 00:12:04,100
of respecting your time and your needs.

311
00:12:04,100 --> 00:12:05,500
Mathieu: Not always easy,

312
00:12:05,500 --> 00:12:07,610
especially when you want to please

313
00:12:07,610 --> 00:12:08,740
everyone.

314
00:12:08,790 --> 00:12:09,730
Léa: That's true.

315
00:12:09,730 --> 00:12:13,900
But sometimes, a simple "I'll think about it"

316
00:12:13,900 --> 00:12:16,460
saves us from immediately accepting

317
00:12:16,460 --> 00:12:18,400
a new obligation.

318
00:12:18,400 --> 00:12:20,400
Mathieu: I really like that phrase.

319
00:12:20,400 --> 00:12:22,340
It's polite, natural,

320
00:12:22,340 --> 00:12:25,400
and it gives us time to choose.

321
00:12:25,400 --> 00:12:28,690
Léa: You see, changing your life can sometimes start

322
00:12:28,690 --> 00:12:32,500
by changing a single habitual answer.

323
00:12:32,500 --> 00:12:33,870
Me too.

324
00:12:33,920 --> 00:12:36,620
We want to eat better, do more sport,

325
00:12:36,620 --> 00:12:38,150
learn a language,

326
00:12:38,150 --> 00:12:41,400
read more, be more organised...

327
00:12:41,400 --> 00:12:44,700
And we decide to start everything on Monday.

328
00:12:44,700 --> 00:12:46,430
Mathieu: Then Wednesday comes,

329
00:12:46,430 --> 00:12:49,100
and the whole plan falls apart.

330
00:12:49,100 --> 00:12:53,200
Léa: So let's choose one important area instead.

331
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:57,300
A habit, a skill or a project.

332
00:12:57,300 --> 00:12:59,210
Something concrete

333
00:12:59,210 --> 00:13:02,470
that we can try for a while.

334
00:13:02,520 --> 00:13:04,800
Mathieu: For example, if I want to rediscover

335
00:13:04,800 --> 00:13:07,000
the joy of taking photos,

336
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,470
I can set aside a short walk

337
00:13:09,470 --> 00:13:11,940
every Saturday, rather than promising

338
00:13:11,940 --> 00:13:14,700
to take a hundred photos a day.

339
00:13:14,700 --> 00:13:17,600
Léa: Exactly. And if you'd like

340
00:13:17,600 --> 00:13:19,900
to spend more time with your friends,

341
00:13:19,900 --> 00:13:22,900
you can start by suggesting a lunch,

342
00:13:22,900 --> 00:13:23,930
instead of waiting

343
00:13:23,930 --> 00:13:26,500
for a get-together to happen by itself.

344
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:27,900
Mathieu: I like that idea.

345
00:13:27,900 --> 00:13:31,770
A modest action, but chosen consciously.

346
00:13:31,820 --> 00:13:33,260
Léa: And if it doesn't work,

347
00:13:33,260 --> 00:13:35,400
it's not necessarily a disaster.

348
00:13:35,400 --> 00:13:37,310
You can understand why,

349
00:13:37,310 --> 00:13:40,400
then adapt your approach.

350
00:13:40,400 --> 00:13:43,300
Mathieu: In fact, change looks less like

351
00:13:43,300 --> 00:13:45,800
a big magical transformation

352
00:13:45,800 --> 00:13:48,800
than a series of choices and adjustments.

353
00:13:48,800 --> 00:13:50,000
Léa: Absolutely.

354
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,800
And these choices don't have to be perfect

355
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:55,000
to teach us something.

356
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,110
Mathieu: You know, I have another story

357
00:13:57,110 --> 00:13:58,870
that comes to mind.

358
00:13:58,920 --> 00:14:02,100
In my neighbourhood, a bookseller wanted

359
00:14:02,100 --> 00:14:05,200
to start a little book club for years.

360
00:14:05,200 --> 00:14:07,150
Léa: What was stopping her from starting?

361
00:14:07,200 --> 00:14:09,500
Mathieu: She imagined a big event,

362
00:14:09,500 --> 00:14:11,400
with lots of participants,

363
00:14:11,400 --> 00:14:15,400
a guest author and complicated planning.

364
00:14:15,400 --> 00:14:18,700
So she kept putting her idea off.

365
00:14:18,700 --> 00:14:20,150
Léa: And in the end?

366
00:14:20,200 --> 00:14:21,400
Mathieu: One Friday,

367
00:14:21,400 --> 00:14:24,100
she simply invited a few regulars

368
00:14:24,100 --> 00:14:27,270
to discuss a novel around a table.

369
00:14:27,320 --> 00:14:30,800
There were five of them, with tea and biscuits.

370
00:14:30,800 --> 00:14:33,100
Léa: That sounds really warm and friendly.

371
00:14:33,100 --> 00:14:35,040
Mathieu: Yes. Some meetings

372
00:14:35,040 --> 00:14:36,800
attracted few people,

373
00:14:36,800 --> 00:14:38,800
others more.

374
00:14:38,800 --> 00:14:40,140
But she discovered

375
00:14:40,140 --> 00:14:42,800
that the joy was in the conversation,

376
00:14:42,800 --> 00:14:45,000
not in the size of the event.

377
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,600
Léa: That's a really lovely story.

378
00:14:47,600 --> 00:14:50,230
It shows we can make new room

379
00:14:50,230 --> 00:14:52,810
in our lives for something that matters,

380
00:14:52,810 --> 00:14:55,170
without necessarily replacing everything.

381
00:14:55,220 --> 00:14:56,830
Mathieu: And sometimes we discover

382
00:14:56,830 --> 00:14:58,620
that the hardest part was

383
00:14:58,620 --> 00:15:01,800
daring to organise the first meeting.

384
00:15:01,800 --> 00:15:03,870
Léa: Exactly. The first step

385
00:15:03,870 --> 00:15:05,900
doesn't guarantee the result,

386
00:15:05,900 --> 00:15:08,010
but it gives us an experience

387
00:15:08,010 --> 00:15:11,000
we can reflect on.

388
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,510
Mathieu: So, if our listeners are hesitating

389
00:15:13,510 --> 00:15:15,200
to start a project,

390
00:15:15,200 --> 00:15:16,680
maybe they can look for

391
00:15:16,680 --> 00:15:18,100
a small experiment

392
00:15:18,100 --> 00:15:19,600
without much risk,

393
00:15:19,600 --> 00:15:22,370
rather than a final decision.

394
00:15:22,420 --> 00:15:23,660
Léa: Yes. And if they have

395
00:15:23,660 --> 00:15:26,100
important responsibilities,

396
00:15:26,100 --> 00:15:28,200
they can move forward carefully,

397
00:15:28,200 --> 00:15:29,680
prepare things

398
00:15:29,680 --> 00:15:32,900
and ask for advice when necessary.

399
00:15:32,900 --> 00:15:35,200
Mathieu: OK, Léa, we've used

400
00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:37,800
lots of French expressions today.

401
00:15:37,800 --> 00:15:40,350
Could we review a few of them?

402
00:15:40,400 --> 00:15:42,050
Léa: With pleasure.

403
00:15:42,100 --> 00:15:45,650
Let's start with "prendre un nouveau départ".

404
00:15:45,700 --> 00:15:48,670
It means starting a new chapter,

405
00:15:48,720 --> 00:15:49,430
for example

406
00:15:49,430 --> 00:15:52,900
after a professional or personal change.

407
00:15:52,900 --> 00:15:54,600
Mathieu: As in this sentence:

408
00:15:54,600 --> 00:15:57,300
"After several years in the same field,

409
00:15:57,300 --> 00:16:00,700
she decided to make a fresh start."

410
00:16:00,700 --> 00:16:03,200
Léa: Very good. Second expression:

411
00:16:03,200 --> 00:16:06,000
"ne plus se sentir à sa place".

412
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,400
It describes the feeling that a setting

413
00:16:08,400 --> 00:16:10,700
no longer really suits us.

414
00:16:10,700 --> 00:16:13,200
Mathieu: "I learned a lot at this company,

415
00:16:13,200 --> 00:16:16,570
but I don't really feel I belong there anymore."

416
00:16:16,620 --> 00:16:19,600
Léa: Exactly. Third expression:

417
00:16:19,600 --> 00:16:21,500
"remettre en question".

418
00:16:21,500 --> 00:16:25,200
It means rethinking an idea

419
00:16:25,200 --> 00:16:26,380
or a habit

420
00:16:26,380 --> 00:16:29,700
we used to accept without thinking.

421
00:16:29,700 --> 00:16:31,570
Mathieu: "This experience led me

422
00:16:31,570 --> 00:16:32,900
to question

423
00:16:32,900 --> 00:16:35,100
some of my priorities."

424
00:16:35,100 --> 00:16:38,100
Léa: Fourth expression: "se lancer".

425
00:16:38,100 --> 00:16:40,850
It means starting an activity

426
00:16:40,850 --> 00:16:44,730
or a project, sometimes despite hesitations.

427
00:16:44,780 --> 00:16:47,040
Mathieu: "I'd love to learn to sing,

428
00:16:47,040 --> 00:16:49,300
but I'm still hesitant to take the plunge."

429
00:16:49,300 --> 00:16:52,800
Léa: Fifth expression: "changer de cap".

430
00:16:52,800 --> 00:16:55,350
It means changing the direction

431
00:16:55,350 --> 00:16:56,300
you're following,

432
00:16:56,300 --> 00:16:59,600
often in your career or your projects.

433
00:16:59,600 --> 00:17:01,510
Mathieu: "I decided to change course

434
00:17:01,510 --> 00:17:03,600
and explore a new profession."

435
00:17:03,600 --> 00:17:07,300
Léa: Sixth expression: "prendre du recul".

436
00:17:07,300 --> 00:17:10,200
It means looking at a situation

437
00:17:10,200 --> 00:17:11,870
with a bit of distance

438
00:17:11,920 --> 00:17:15,800
before reacting or making a decision.

439
00:17:15,800 --> 00:17:17,300
Mathieu: "I was very disappointed,

440
00:17:17,300 --> 00:17:19,700
so I preferred to step back

441
00:17:19,700 --> 00:17:23,000
before deciding what to do next."

442
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:24,800
Léa: And one last expression:

443
00:17:24,800 --> 00:17:27,100
"faire le premier pas".

444
00:17:27,100 --> 00:17:29,060
It means taking

445
00:17:29,060 --> 00:17:32,500
the first action towards a goal.

446
00:17:32,500 --> 00:17:34,800
Mathieu: "I've talked about this project for a long time.

447
00:17:34,800 --> 00:17:37,220
Today, I'm finally ready

448
00:17:37,220 --> 00:17:39,170
to take the first step."

449
00:17:39,220 --> 00:17:41,900
Léa: Those are very useful expressions.

450
00:17:41,900 --> 00:17:43,300
And now, what if we made

451
00:17:43,300 --> 00:17:46,200
a few sentences sound more natural?

452
00:17:46,200 --> 00:17:49,300
Mathieu: Good idea! First sentence:

453
00:17:49,300 --> 00:17:51,500
"I want to change my life."

454
00:17:51,500 --> 00:17:52,800
You can say:

455
00:17:52,800 --> 00:17:56,000
"I feel I need a change."

456
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:57,910
Léa: Or: "I'd like to give

457
00:17:57,910 --> 00:18:00,200
a new direction to my daily life."

458
00:18:00,200 --> 00:18:02,790
It's a less dramatic way

459
00:18:02,790 --> 00:18:04,600
of presenting your wish.

460
00:18:04,600 --> 00:18:08,970
Mathieu: Second sentence: "I don't like my job anymore."

461
00:18:09,020 --> 00:18:10,100
You can say:

462
00:18:10,100 --> 00:18:12,180
"I don't really feel

463
00:18:12,180 --> 00:18:14,500
I belong in this job anymore."

464
00:18:14,500 --> 00:18:18,200
Léa: Third sentence: "I'm scared to start."

465
00:18:18,200 --> 00:18:19,560
You can say:

466
00:18:19,560 --> 00:18:21,800
"I'm still hesitant to take the plunge." Or:

467
00:18:21,800 --> 00:18:25,000
"I'm a little nervous about the first step."

468
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,900
Mathieu: "Appréhender" means feeling

469
00:18:27,900 --> 00:18:31,000
worry before a situation.

470
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,680
For example: "I'm dreading

471
00:18:33,680 --> 00:18:36,700
my first job interview in French."

472
00:18:36,700 --> 00:18:40,200
Léa: Fourth sentence: "I've changed my mind."

473
00:18:40,200 --> 00:18:41,230
You can say:

474
00:18:41,230 --> 00:18:45,100
"Over time, my priorities have changed."

475
00:18:45,100 --> 00:18:47,010
Mathieu: Or: "What I wanted

476
00:18:47,010 --> 00:18:50,900
in the past no longer suits me today."

477
00:18:50,900 --> 00:18:54,600
Léa: Fifth sentence: "I don't know what to do."

478
00:18:54,600 --> 00:18:55,650
You can say:

479
00:18:55,650 --> 00:18:58,360
"I need to step back

480
00:18:58,360 --> 00:19:00,200
before deciding."

481
00:19:00,200 --> 00:19:02,080
Mathieu: And the last one:

482
00:19:02,080 --> 00:19:04,770
"I'm going to try something new."

483
00:19:04,820 --> 00:19:05,550
You can say:

484
00:19:05,550 --> 00:19:07,140
"I feel like exploring

485
00:19:07,140 --> 00:19:09,400
new possibilities."

486
00:19:09,400 --> 00:19:11,700
Léa: Exactly. All these expressions

487
00:19:11,700 --> 00:19:13,000
let us talk

488
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:17,100
about our choices with more precision and nuance.

489
00:19:17,100 --> 00:19:19,900
Mathieu: You know, Léa, after this conversation,

490
00:19:19,900 --> 00:19:22,800
I think I've understood something.

491
00:19:22,800 --> 00:19:25,650
Changing your life doesn't necessarily mean

492
00:19:25,650 --> 00:19:28,170
throwing away everything you've built.

493
00:19:28,220 --> 00:19:30,940
Léa: No. We can keep what does us good,

494
00:19:30,940 --> 00:19:33,360
change what no longer suits us

495
00:19:33,360 --> 00:19:37,200
and try new things, gradually.

496
00:19:37,200 --> 00:19:38,230
Mathieu: And we're allowed

497
00:19:38,230 --> 00:19:41,000
not to know everything that comes next.

498
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:43,960
Sometimes a small experiment is enough

499
00:19:43,960 --> 00:19:47,700
to discover an interesting possibility.

500
00:19:47,700 --> 00:19:50,170
Léa: Exactly. We don't need

501
00:19:50,170 --> 00:19:53,000
a final answer to every question.

502
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:55,870
We can learn as we go.

503
00:19:55,920 --> 00:19:57,710
Mathieu: So, this week,

504
00:19:57,710 --> 00:20:00,400
I'm going to take my camera out again.

505
00:20:00,400 --> 00:20:02,470
A walk on Saturday morning,

506
00:20:02,470 --> 00:20:04,900
with no particular goal.

507
00:20:04,900 --> 00:20:06,900
Léa: That's an excellent idea!

508
00:20:06,900 --> 00:20:08,270
And I'm going to keep

509
00:20:08,270 --> 00:20:10,040
an evening free of plans

510
00:20:10,040 --> 00:20:12,700
to read or cook in peace.

511
00:20:12,700 --> 00:20:14,650
Mathieu: An evening with no plans?

512
00:20:14,700 --> 00:20:16,550
Can I come?

513
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:18,330
Léa: Mathieu, if I invite you,

514
00:20:18,330 --> 00:20:21,100
it won't be an evening with no plans anymore!

515
00:20:21,100 --> 00:20:25,170
Mathieu: Fair point. I'll respect your new project.

516
00:20:25,220 --> 00:20:26,500
Léa: And you, dear friends,

517
00:20:26,500 --> 00:20:28,920
what little thing would you like to change

518
00:20:28,920 --> 00:20:29,950
in your life?

519
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,300
Maybe getting back to an activity,

520
00:20:32,300 --> 00:20:33,800
learning something

521
00:20:33,800 --> 00:20:36,850
or simply finding more time for yourself.

522
00:20:36,900 --> 00:20:39,670
Mathieu: In the comments, you can write:

523
00:20:39,720 --> 00:20:42,870
"This year, I'd like to change..." Or:

524
00:20:42,920 --> 00:20:45,260
"I want to get started with..."

525
00:20:45,300 --> 00:20:46,750
A single sentence is enough

526
00:20:46,750 --> 00:20:48,800
to practise your French.

527
00:20:48,800 --> 00:20:50,710
Léa: Don't worry if you make

528
00:20:50,710 --> 00:20:51,500
a few mistakes.

529
00:20:51,500 --> 00:20:54,370
We're here to learn together.

530
00:20:54,420 --> 00:20:56,410
And if you enjoyed this episode,

531
00:20:56,410 --> 00:20:58,500
remember to leave a little thumbs-up

532
00:20:58,500 --> 00:21:01,500
and subscribe to The Cozy French.

533
00:21:01,500 --> 00:21:04,500
Mathieu: Thank you for sharing this moment with us.

534
00:21:04,500 --> 00:21:05,990
Take care of yourselves,

535
00:21:05,990 --> 00:21:08,680
and remember that a change doesn't need

536
00:21:08,680 --> 00:21:12,100
to impress others to be important.

537
00:21:12,100 --> 00:21:13,460
Léa: Sometimes it's enough

538
00:21:13,460 --> 00:21:15,600
to recognise what we're missing,

539
00:21:15,600 --> 00:21:17,660
then give it a little place

540
00:21:17,660 --> 00:21:19,200
in our daily life.

541
00:21:19,200 --> 00:21:21,110
Go at your own pace,

542
00:21:21,110 --> 00:21:24,140
with curiosity and kindness.

543
00:21:24,190 --> 00:21:25,400
Mathieu: This was Mathieu...

544
00:21:25,400 --> 00:21:27,400
Léa: And Léa, from The Cozy French!

545
00:21:27,400 --> 00:21:29,400
Mathieu: See you very soon

546
00:21:29,400 --> 00:21:32,600
for a new conversation in French.

547
00:21:32,600 --> 00:21:34,600
Léa: See you very soon, dear friends.

548
00:21:34,600 --> 00:21:36,600
And take care of yourselves!

549
00:21:36,600 --> 00:21:38,800
Mathieu: See you soon!
